<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.15.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01524</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.15.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d8009569-1b90-40b2-a4f0-3fe5c8d979c5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson. Double click on mathematical
  expressions/equations to enlarge. If needed, use the “^” symbol to enter an exponent.</para>
<!--Do not edit this div tag.  It must surround the interaction block-->
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="746ff831-1c97-402c-859e-a02d9ebd63cd" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="54828607-798d-48f4-9098-8816f83d3f26" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(f(x)=5x+3\)" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)=3x+5\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)=5x+3\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( f(x)=5^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\( f(x)=x^5\\) &quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Whenever the input of a function \(f\) increases by 1, the output increases by 5. Which of these equations
          could define \(f\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="4c75059f-e45f-4203-9d6d-2787a3c8b931" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;When the input \\(x\\) increases by 1, the value of \\(f\\) increases by a factor of 2.&quot;, &quot;When the input \\(x\\) increases by 3, the value of \\(f\\) increases by a factor of 8.&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;When the input \\(x\\) increases by 1, the value of \\(f\\) increases by 2.&quot;, &quot;When the input \\(x\\) increases by 1, the value of \\(f\\) increases by a factor of 2.&quot;, &quot;When the input \\(x\\) increases by 3, the value of \\(f\\) increases by 8.&quot;, &quot;When the input \\(x\\) increases by 3, the value of \\(f\\) increases by a factor of 8.&quot;, &quot;When the input \\(x\\) increases by 4, the value of \\(f\\) increases by a factor of 4.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The function \(f\) is defined by \(f(x)=2^x\). Which of the following statements is true about the values of
          \(f\)? <emphasis>Select all </emphasis>that apply. </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="968c16ab-3a12-494b-9d43-4b32752c1cab" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="(0,0) and (1,2) are two points on the graph of \(f\). Between the two points, \(y\) increases by 2 and \(x\) increases by 1." data-solution-options="[&quot;The \(y\)-axis counts by 2’s so f must increase 2 each time the input increases 1.&quot;, &quot;There are two graphs so f increases by 2 each time the input \\(x\\) increases by 1.&quot;, &quot;(0,0) and (1,2) are two points on the graph of \\(f\\). Between the two points, \\(y\\) increases by 2 and \\(x\\) increases by 1.&quot;, &quot;\\(f\\) does not increase by 2 each time \\(x\\) increases by 1.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The two lines on the coordinate plane are graphs of functions \(f\) and \(g\). Use for the graph below for
        questions 3 and 4.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Graph of 2 lines."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/12724ad9231ed2d7dd1720cc90447eb9c5fa3e21"/></media></figure></para>
      <para>Use the graph to explain why the value of \(f\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by
          1. 
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a146c478-5f35-4970-b07d-8d9594463256" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\(f\\) and \\(g\\) never intersect and stay an equal distance apart. \\(f\\) increases by 2 every time x increases by 1, so \\(g\\) must do so as well.&quot;, &quot;(0,5) and (1,7) are points on the graph of \\(g\\).  Between the two points, \\(y\\) increases by 2 and \\(x\\) increases by 1.&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f\\) and \\(g\\) never intersect and stay an equal distance apart. \\(f\\) increases by 2 every time x increases by 1, so \\(g\\) must do so as well.&quot;, &quot;You can’t use the graph because the points are not labeled.&quot;, &quot;There are two graphs so \\(g\\) increases by 2 each time the input \\(x\\) increases by 1.&quot;, &quot;(0,5) and (1,7) are points on the graph of \\(g\\).  Between the two points, \\(y\\) increases by 2 and \\(x\\) increases by 1.&quot;, &quot;(0,0) and (1,2) are two points on the graph of \\(g\\).  Between the two points, \\(y\\) increases by 2 and \\(x\\) increases by 1.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Use the graph to explain why the value of \(g\) increases by 2 each time the input \(x\) increases by 1.
          <emphasis>Select two </emphasis>that apply.
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.2.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="1107dbf8-66fc-45a5-9745-97e5975d3473" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="25">
    <problem>
      <para>The function \(h\) is given by \(h(x)=5^x\). Find the quotient \(\frac{h(x+2)}{h(x)}\). 
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.3. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="5674259f-b084-4426-bbff-5cf458fbcbd1" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="125" data-solution-options="[&quot;5&quot;, &quot;15&quot;, &quot;25&quot;, &quot;125&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The function \(h\) is given by \(h(x)=5^x\). Find the quotient \(\frac{h(x+3)}{h(x)}\).</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="68556bd3-af50-4ef9-b72b-3b62d0a83449" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\( f(x)=x+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(h(x)=111x−23\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(q(x)=87.5\\)&quot; ]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\( f(x)=x+2\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(g(x)=2^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(h(x)=111x−23\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(p(x)=50,000 \\cdot 3^x\\)&quot;, &quot;\\(q(x)=87.5\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>For each of the functions \(f,g,h,p\), and \(q\), the domain is \(0≤x≤100\). For which functions is
          the average rate of change a good measure of how the function changes for this domain? <emphasis>Select three</emphasis> that
          apply. </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.4. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="2ba83d50-cf33-400c-a317-9a186fa46fc3" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2">
    <problem>
      <para>What would be the rate of change of the following table?</para><table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
                \(x\)
              </entry>
              <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
                \(f(x)\)
              </entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                1
              </entry>
              <entry>
                -4
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                2
              </entry>
              <entry>
                -2
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                3
              </entry>
              <entry>
                0
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                4
              </entry>
              <entry>
                2
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                5
              </entry>
              <entry>
                4
              </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.15.2.
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9c739c4f-ee63-4f46-bfdd-8ccfeb05fbc7" data-problem-comparator="integer" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2">
    <problem>
      <para>Given the following table, what would be \(\frac {f(x+2)}{f(x+1)}\)?</para>
        <table class="os-raise-skinnytable">
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
                \(x\)
              </entry>
              <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">
                \(f(x)\)
              </entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>
                1
              </entry>
              <entry>
                2
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                2
              </entry>
              <entry>
                4
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                3
              </entry>
              <entry>
                8
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                4
              </entry>
              <entry>
                16
              </entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>
                5
              </entry>
              <entry>
                32
              </entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.15.3. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="bf41e467-06b4-41c1-8496-accddf0ee0a0" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="In \(f\) you add 3 to get to the next term; in \(g\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term." data-solution-options="[&quot;In \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.&quot;, &quot;In \\(f\\) and \\(g\\) you add 3 to get to the next term.&quot;, &quot;In \\(f\\) you add 3 to get to the next term; in \\(g\\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.&quot;, &quot;In \\(g\\) you add 3 to get to the next term; in \\(f\\) you multiply by 3 to get to the next term.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following statements compare rates of \(f(x) = 3x +2\) and \(g(x)=3^x\)?</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try Again!</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Do not edit below this line-->
  <solution> </solution>
  <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response"> </div>
</div></section>
